<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="superoxide radicals degradation" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="DETOX1-PWY" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; All organisms living in an aerobic environment are exposed to reactive oxygen species (ROS) that are formed through metabolic processes and various environmental stresses such as drought, air pollutants, UV light and high light intensities, chilling temperatures and external chemicals |CITS: [VANBREUSGEM99][11997379]|. For example, active oxygen species are produced during the &amp;beta;-oxidation of fatty acids or as a result of photorespiration in photosynthetic organisms |CITS: [8819328]|. ROS such as |FRAME: SUPER-OXIDE| and hydroxyl radicals as well as |FRAME: HYDROGEN-PEROXIDE| can cause significant damage to proteins, nucleic acids and cell organelles. Most of the aerobic organisms have developed defense systems to face oxidative stress and to scavenge oxidative radicals in the form of enzymes that can detoxify ROS, such as |FRAME: SUPEROX-DISMUTMN-ENZRXN| (SOD) and |FRAME: HYDROPEROXIDI-ENZRXN| (CAT) |CITS: [BEYER87]|. For example, |FRAME: TAX-3702|, a member of the mustard family (oilseed plants), stores energy reserves preliminary as lipids that undergo &amp;beta;-oxidation during germination. The |FRAME: HYDROGEN-PEROXIDE| that is produced during this metabolic process is detoxified by catalase. Another form of ROS, |FRAME: SUPER-OXIDE| radicals, are by-products of aerobic electron transfer chains, and are disposed of by the action of superoxide dismutase. Since ROS can be found in any compartment of the eukaryotic cell, organisms have developed small gene families encoding for several SOD and CAT enzymes that operate in the various cell compartments |CITS: [9765550][9214587]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; SODs represent the first line of defense against ROS, converting |FRAME: SUPER-OXIDE| radicals to |FRAME: HYDROGEN-PEROXIDE| and water. SODs are differentiated with regard to their metal cofactor. There are iron-dependent, manganese-dependent and copper/zinc-dependent SODs, which differ not only in their metal cofactor but also in their subcellular location. In plants, FeSODs are located in chloroplasts and are regarded the most ancient SOD group. MnSODs are found in the mitochondrion and the peroxisome and are structurally very similar to FeSODs. The last group, the Cu-ZnSODs operates in chloroplasts, the cytosol and even the extracellular space. They are structurally very different from the other two SOD groups because of the different electrical properties of copper in comparison to iron or manganese, which resulted in a major structural change in the protein |CITS: [11997379]|. To date seven SODs have been identified in |FRAME: TAX-3702|, three of them iron-dependent, three having copper as metal cofactor and one manganese-dependent SOD |CITS: [1731963][2263641][9765550]|. It has been demonstrated that a copper-chaperone (AtCCS, At1g12520) is crucial for the activation of all three Cu/Zn-dependent SODs in this organism. The SOD holoenzyme usually constitutes either a homodimer or a homotetramer. However, the exact composition of the SODs in Arabidopsis is currently not known and remains to be verified (here displayed as polypeptides). Catalase is second in the defense line against active oxygen, converting |FRAME: HYDROGEN-PEROXIDE| into water and oxygen. Three genes encoding subunits of catalase and at least 6 catalase isoenzymes have been identified in Arabidopsis so far |CITS: [12232134][9214587][8819328][8668130]|. Besides their implication in detoxifying ROS, catalases are thought to play a role in the signal transduction pathway in plants leading to the development of SAR (systemic aquired resistance) |CITS: [7953568]|. The functional protein of catalase is a tetramer but the question whether it exists as homo- or heterotetramer of different subunits remains to be investigated.</Description>
